// TEST: Idx Card Self Reference (rejects)
// ============================================================================
// A STRUCT'S SOLUTION COUNT CANNOT BE ONE OF THE THINGS ITS CONSTRAINTS
// DEPEND ON.
//
//   conjunct 1 of R did not fold: idx_card(R): counting R requires folding
//   R's own constraints, which reach idx_card(R) again - a struct's solution
//   count cannot be one of the things its constraints depend on. Currently
//   being counted: R at (p = 0)
//
// The circularity is real rather than merely awkward: `idx_card(R)` is
// defined as the number of box cells whose conjuncts hold, so a conjunct
// that reads it is a fixed-point equation the counting layer has no way to
// solve and never claimed to. §2.4's assert-not-solve ruling settles what to
// do about it — refuse, and say why.
//
// WHY THIS NEEDS ITS OWN GUARD, when 156 next door is caught by the
// evaluator's depth budget. `idx_card` is a SYNTACTIC static builtin, and
// `registerSyntacticStaticBuiltin` hands a handler a step COUNT rather than
// the live fold — the handler is a compiler subroutine with its own cost
// model (this one folds a conjunct per box cell, which has no expression in
// the caller's remaining steps), so it necessarily starts a fresh budget.
// Fresh budget means depth zero, so every hop through `idx_card` resets the
// counter and the recursion is invisible to the guard that catches 156. The
// evaluator cannot see this cycle and it would be dishonest to make it look
// as though it could; the layer that owns the re-entry owns the guard, which
// is `StructIdxSpec`'s enumeration-in-progress set.
//
// THE GUARD IS KEYED ON THE STRUCT NAME, NOT ON SELF-REFERENCE, so an
// indirect cycle — R's conjunct counts S, S's conjunct counts R — is caught
// by the same check and reported the same way. Self-reference is just the
// shortest instance, and it is the one written here because a two-struct
// version would pin the same mechanism at twice the length.
//
// This program crashed the compiler before the guard existed, and it did so
// for the same reason the fuel bomb in 156 did: an unbounded recursion that
// no budget was watching. That the two needed separate fixes is the finding
// worth keeping — the depth guard is necessary and is not sufficient.
// ============================================================================
static struct R {
    p: Int<min=0, max=2>
} where p == idx_card(R)

let static CARD = idx_card(R)

function main() -> Int = CARD
